{{also|Haven}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|keh₂p}}
From {{inh|en|enm|haven}}, {{m|enm|havene}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hæfen||haven; harbour; port}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*habnō}}, {{m|gem-pro|*habanō}} (compare {{cog|nl|haven}}, {{cog|de|Hafen}}, {{cog|no|-}}/{{cog|da|havn}}), from {{der|en|gem-pro|*habą||sea}} (compare {{cog|ang|hæf}}, {{cog|gml|haf}}, {{cog|non|haf||sea}}, {{cog|de|Haff||bay or lagoon behind a spit}}, perhaps, in the sense of "heaving sea", etymologically identical with {{cog|non|haf||heaving, lifting, uplift, elevation}}, derived from {{cog|gem-pro|*habjaną||to lift, heave}}), or from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*kh₂pnós}} (compare {{cog|sga|cúan||harbor, recess, haven}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈheɪvən/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-haven.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪvən}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[harbour]] or [[anchorage]] protected from the sea.
#* {{rfdatek|en|Shakespeare}}
#*: what shipping and what lading's in our '''haven'''
#* {{rfdatek|en|Tennyson}}
#*: their '''haven''' under the hill
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A place of [[safety]]; a refuge or sanctuary.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 21, 2011|author=Helen Pidd| title=Europeans migrate south as continent drifts deeper into crisis| work=the Guardian| url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2011/dec/21/europe-migrants-crisis-irish-portuguese| passage=Since its conception, the European Union has been a '''haven''' for those seeking refuge from war, persecution and poverty in other parts of the world.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A peaceful place.

